# App Store Rating Classifier

## a. Problem statement
Predict whether an Apple App Store app is "highly rated" (user rating of 4.0 or higher)
from its metadata, using five classification models, and compare them with six metrics.
Results can be explored in a deployed Streamlit app.

## b. Dataset description
- **Source:** Kaggle, Apple App Store apps dataset (AppleStore.csv and appleStore_description.csv). [add the exact Kaggle link you used]
- **Raw size:** 7,197 apps. Apps with zero ratings were removed (an unrated app is not a poorly rated one), leaving **6,268 instances**.
- **Target:** `highly_rated` = 1 if user_rating >= 4.0, else 0 (about 76% positive, so the classes are imbalanced).
- **Features (14):**
  - Numeric (12): size_mb, price, rating_count_tot, rating_count_ver, sup_devices.num, ipadSc_urls.num, lang.num, vpp_lic, desc_len, name_len, ver_major, is_free
  - Categorical (2): cont_rating, prime_genre
- **Engineered features:** desc_len (from the description file), name_len, ver_major, size_mb, is_free.
- **Excluded:** user_rating_ver, because it is the rating of the current version and leaks the target.
- **Split:** 80/20 stratified (random_state=42), 5,014 train and 1,254 test.
- **Preprocessing:** StandardScaler for numeric, one-hot encoding for categorical, saved together with each model as one pipeline.

## c. GitHub repository
https://github.com/Varenyamalik/appstore-rating-classifier

Live app: [paste your streamlit.app URL]

## d. Models used

| ML Model Name | Accuracy | AUC | Precision | Recall | F1 | MCC |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Logistic Regression | 0.770 | 0.742 | 0.790 | 0.953 | 0.864 | 0.213 |
| Decision Tree | 0.751 | 0.716 | 0.812 | 0.878 | 0.843 | 0.248 |
| KNN | 0.761 | 0.651 | 0.806 | 0.905 | 0.852 | 0.245 |
| Naive Bayes | 0.720 | 0.669 | 0.838 | 0.785 | 0.811 | 0.279 |
| Support Vector Machine | 0.762 | 0.738 | 0.773 | 0.974 | 0.862 | 0.120 |

